@@ -0,0 +1,617 @@
+# F-Zero X headless RL and PufferLib 5.0 specification
+
+Status: ABI v3 runtime and Puffer adapter implemented; 83-input one-lap PPO pace baseline passed;
+three-lap, full-action, and exact-simulator scaling work pending, 2026-08-24
+
+This document defines the first trainable reinforcement-learning environment built from
+G-Diffuser. It is deliberately a correctness contract before it is a performance plan. The CPU
+implementation remains the reference even if selected work later moves to CUDA.
+
+## Pinned baseline
+
+- G-Diffuser upstream base: `c62bf97`; the integration is developed as local commits on the
+  `pufferlib-headless` branch.
+- PufferLib 5.0 upstream base: `0aa034def1b3c91af9971798288f8bddd1817277`; the integration is
+  isolated on a local branch from the `origin/5.0` ref. PufferLib 4.0 trees are reference-only and
+  must not be modified.
+- Game data: North American revision 0, SHA-1
+  `5f658e88ffa9de23cba6986a8fd3d3a90d7b4340`. The ROM is private, untracked, and supplied at
+  runtime. No ROM bytes, extracted game data, or host-specific absolute ROM path belongs in Git;
+  the Puffer config may contain a portable path into the ignored workspace-private directory.
+- First target: Linux x86-64, cartridge-only (`GDX_EXPANSION_KIT=OFF`). Expansion Kit, rendering,
+  audio output, and GPU simulation are out of scope for the first trainable environment.
+
+## What counts as working
+
+The project is not complete merely because a Puffer process launches. The first end-to-end
+milestone must satisfy all of these conditions:
+
+1. The real port advances F-Zero X race physics without creating a window, GL context, audio
+   device, or wall-clock pacing loop.
+2. A reset reaches a controllable one-car Time Attack race with a chosen seed, course, machine,
+   and engine setting.
+3. One environment step applies a documented action for exactly `action_repeat` 60 Hz game
+   ticks and returns a fixed observation, reward, and episode boundary.
+4. Equal seed plus equal action trace produces an equal semantic state hash for at least 10,000
+   physics ticks in two independently loaded instances (fresh processes or ELF namespaces).
+5. The built-in F-Zero CPU controller completes a lap through the same headless step path. This is
+   the initial plumbing and physics oracle.
+6. PufferLib 5.0 performs an actual PPO update and writes a checkpoint. A fresh-process,
+   fixed-sampler reproducible stochastic evaluation must show behavior or return above the random
+   and no-op baselines (and a greedy comparison once argmax evaluation exists). Loss output alone
+   is not evidence of a useful environment.
+7. Performance reports include both policy decisions per second and 60 Hz game ticks per second,
+   together with the action repeat and number of simulator processes.
+
+The first behavior gate after the smoke test is at least 90% successful one-lap Mute City finishes over
+256 reproducible stochastic policy rollouts; the steering-only policy has passed that gate. The
+next behavior target applies the same gate to three laps before the full action space is judged.
+Solo Mute City physics is currently invariant to the episode seed for tested action traces, so
+reset seeds are a reproducibility control, not a held-out generalization set. Generalization claims
+require explicit start-state, machine, or course variants.
+
+## Architecture decision
+
+The game is dominated by mutable global state, a singleton scheduler, and host fibers. Version 1
+therefore supports exactly one live F-Zero runtime per loaded ELF namespace. It must not pretend
+that copying an `Env` struct creates an independent game.
+
+```text
+PufferLib 5.0 native trainer
+  -> thin ocean/fzero adapter (Puffer buffers and auto-reset)
+  -> versioned C ABI
+  -> one headless G-Diffuser runtime
+  -> decomp game state, scheduler, course, and racer physics
+```
+
+The reference integration loads `libgdiffuser_rl.so` with Linux `dlmopen(LM_ID_NEWLM, ...)`.
+Two namespaces have been verified to remain independent and bit-exact for more than 10,000 physics
+ticks. The first Puffer smoke still uses one agent, buffer, and worker. Loader namespaces can scale
+that without adding IPC, but glibc's finite namespace limit makes this a small-vector backend, not
+an unlimited one. If processes are later needed, use `posix_spawn`, not `fork` after CUDA
+initialization.
+
+Do not compile the legacy game sources through `nvcc`. Build the runtime with its existing C/C++
+toolchain and expose a narrow `extern "C"` surface. The Puffer binary links the resulting library or
+talks to the server.
+
+The implemented public C contract is `port/rl/gdx_rl_env.h`. Its stable shape is:
+
+```c
+typedef struct {
+    int32_t course_index;
+    int32_t character;
+    int32_t machine_skin;
+    int32_t laps;
+    int32_t action_repeat;
+    int32_t max_episode_frames;
+    int32_t stall_frames;
+    float stall_progress;
+    float engine_balance;
+    uint8_t cpu_oracle;
+} GdxRlConfig;
+```
+
+`stall_progress` is a finite, nonnegative distance threshold. Setting it to zero disables stall
+truncation because every frame refreshes the progress watchdog.
+
+`gdx_rl_abi_version()` returns 3. Init, explicit sequential thread bind, reset, step, semantic hash,
+and error-query functions are exported. Observation and action sizes are compile-time constants;
+the five action cardinalities are also exported as `gdx_rl_action_nvec`. A namespace is intentionally
+process-lifetime: live cooperative contexts make `dlclose` unsafe, so the ABI has no misleading close
+operation. After reset/warmup, the step hot path uses preallocated storage, never sleeps or opens a
+device, and never relies on the host frame rate.
+
+## Headless runtime contract
+
+### Build and boot
+
+Add a dedicated headless target; do not depend on `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy`. The current SDL backend
+requires a visible OpenGL window and cannot be the correctness path.
+
+The headless target must:
+
+- compile the cartridge sources only;
+- bypass interactive first boot and the unconditional disk/IPL checks;
+- accept only an explicit raw ROM path in the core ABI; the Puffer adapter may resolve
+  `FZEROX_ROM` and pass that explicit path;
+- initialize RDRAM, raw-ROM segment access, the cooperative scheduler, and the game;
+- omit the window, ControlDeck, GUI, renderer execution, Discord, file-drop, audio device/thread, present,
+  interpolation, and pacing paths;
+- replace `osGetTime`, `osSetTime`, `osGetCount`, and timer APIs with a deterministic virtual clock
+  advanced exactly once per simulated VI, and bypass the approximately 3.8 second boot-logo wait;
+- use deterministic in-memory SRAM and disable save/ghost persistence so training never reads or
+  changes a user's save;
+- keep graphics and audio scheduler completion semantics so no game fiber waits forever.
+
+There are two implementation stages. First, get a linkable cartridge-only logic target with
+minimal host shims and no libultraship runtime dependency. Keep the game's update and draw-list
+generation, but discard renderer execution, presentation, and audio output. This will expose
+whether the game can boot entirely from raw ROM data. If archive-backed assets prove necessary
+before the race can initialize, the fallback is a resource-only libultraship bootstrap with no
+Window or Audio subsystem. It is still headless at runtime, but it is not the desired final
+dependency boundary.
+
+The renderer-independent target still needs correct host-address and asset resolution. Factor the
+CPU-only `Gdx_ResolvePortAddress` behavior out of `port/n64_gfx_bridge.cpp`, preserving registered
+host ranges, RDRAM/ROM reconstruction, wide generated-asset placeholders, and segment mappings.
+This cannot be replaced by a blanket null stub: bad low-32/KSEG reconstruction can silently route
+course DMA to the wrong memory.
+
+Retain draw-list generation for the first oracle because game draw functions mutate live state;
+`Race_Draw`, for example, advances a race timer, and draw updates `machineLod`, which damage/crash
+behavior reads. The headless scheduler discards execution of the completed display list and posts
+the expected task acknowledgements. Only remove draw generation after a semantic-hash parity test
+proves the optimization safe.
+
+Graphics and audio tasks must still post the completion messages expected by the N64 scheduler.
+Simply returning without SP/DP completion will deadlock the fibers.
+
+### Step ordering
+
+A physics tick uses the port's existing boundary order:
+
+1. Publish the raw N64 pad for port 1.
+2. Run `gdx_controller_poll()` so current, pressed, released, and retrigger fields are derived by
+   the same code as physical and scripted input.
+3. Run `gdx_vi_tick()`; the retrace wakes and executes the game fiber synchronously.
+4. Run `gdx_sched_drain_deferred_wakes()` and `gdx_dispatch()` to preserve scheduler queue
+   semantics.
+5. Collect reward components and semantic state after the game update.
+
+The injection seam is the raw `buttons`, `stick_x`, and `stick_y` pad immediately before
+`gdx_update_port_inputs()` in `port/input_bridge.c`. The ABI reaches it through
+`gdx_headless_set_pad`; legacy cwd `gdx-autoinput.txt` and `GDX_INPUT_SCRIPT` overrides are compiled
+out of `GDX_RL_HEADLESS` so inherited host state cannot replace policy actions. Do not write
+`Racer` control fields directly.
+
+The game runs at 60 physics ticks per second. Default `action_repeat` is 4, giving 15 policy
+decisions per simulated second. Sweep repeats 2, 4, and 8 only after repeat 4 is correct.
+
+### Race start and reset
+
+Use one-player `GAMEMODE_TIME_ATTACK`, no ghost, Mute City, Captain Falcon/Blue Falcon, engine tune
+0.5, and one lap for the plumbing oracle and first learning curriculum. Time Attack produces one
+racer; Practice produces 30 and loops completed laps, so Practice is not the MVP substrate. Move
+to three laps for the full baseline after the one-lap policy finishes reliably.
+
+Enter the race through the normal queued game-mode transition so course, overlays, arenas, and
+`Race_Init` run in their intended order. For reset, the host posts a request only at a quiescent ABI
+boundary; the game fiber consumes it at the top of `Race_Update` and calls the stock retry rebuild
+helper `func_i2_80103A70`. This preserves game-fiber ownership without paying the menu/visual
+transition and never calls `Race_Init` asynchronously from the host.
+
+The first oracle may fast-forward the intro with neutral input until the game enables controls.
+The optimized reset may set `gRaceIntroTimer` to zero only when it also sets the
+`RACER_STATE_FLAGS_400000` control gate. It must be parity-tested against the first controllable
+state reached by neutral fast-forward.
+
+Apply the episode seed at the top of race initialization, before any racer or CPU initialization:
+
+```c
+Math_Rand1Init(seed, seed + seed);
+Math_Rand2Init(seed + seed, seed);
+```
+
+Reset the RL adapter's previous action/pad state and the gameplay frame counter. Do not rerun
+`bootproc` on episode reset: the scheduler's thread registry and Context are process-lifetime
+singletons.
+
+## Action space
+
+The trainable MVP is factorized categorical input:
+
+```text
+MultiDiscrete([11, 5, 2, 2, 2])
+                 |  |  |  |  +-- boost tap (B)
+                 |  |  |  +----- brake held (C-down)
+                 |  |  +-------- throttle held (A)
+                 |  +----------- pitch / stick Y
+                 +-------------- steering / stick X
+```
+
+Steering uses 11 bins:
+
+```text
+[-63, -50, -38, -25, -13, 0, 13, 25, 38, 50, 63]
+```
+
+The categorical IDs use neutral-first ordering (`0, -13, +13, ... -63, +63`) so an all-zero
+Puffer action buffer is a genuine no-op; the set of available steering values is unchanged.
+
+This is the answer to the initial ten-bin question: discretized steering is a strong first
+baseline, but an odd number is preferable. Ten symmetric values have no exact neutral and force a
+constant steering bias. Eleven gives five left values, neutral, and five right values. The game
+normalizes by 63 and clamps, so larger endpoints add no authority.
+
+PufferLib 5.0's native trainer does not currently support a mixed continuous/discrete policy, so
+continuous steering plus categorical buttons is not a drop-in alternative. It would require
+trainer/interface work or an all-continuous encoding. The 11-bin categorical baseline keeps the
+five control heads native and has now demonstrated genuine learning; 7/11/21 bins remain a later
+measured sweep.
+
+Pitch uses:
+
+```text
+[-63, -32, 0, 32, 63]
+```
+
+Pitch likewise uses neutral-first categorical ordering (`0, -32, +32, -63, +63`).
+
+Throttle and brake are held for every repeated physics tick. A boost choice of one produces B
+pressed/current on repeated tick zero, a release on tick one, and off thereafter. This preserves
+the game's edge-triggered boost rule. Consecutive policy decisions may request consecutive valid
+taps.
+
+The adapter validates that every float action supplied by Puffer is finite, integral, and within
+its head's range. Invalid actions fail loudly with the component and value; they abort training
+instead of silently corrupting a rollout.
+PufferLib 5.0 represents categorical IDs as float32 and uses one concatenated action mask of width
+`sum(ACT_SIZES)`.
+
+The ABI always supports the full five-head space. The first learning curriculum masks pitch to
+neutral, throttle on, brake off, and boost off while leaving all 11 steering bins legal. This
+avoids a known local optimum: a zero-action stall returns almost zero, while exploratory spinouts
+are initially negative. Set `env.steering_curriculum=0` to expose all five heads. In that full mode,
+all actions are legal and unavailable boost is a game-defined no-op. A later dynamic mask may
+disable boost-on while boost is unavailable, but every head must retain at least one legal choice.
+Z/R drift and attacks are deliberately deferred until the car can finish reliably. They can later
+be represented as one four-way shoulder head: none, Z, R, or Z+R.
+
+The plumbing oracle is smaller still: force throttle on, hold pitch neutral, disable brake/boost,
+and expose only the 11 steering choices. This is a diagnostic stage, not the final action space.
+
+## Observation space
+
+Version 2 is a structured `float32[83]` vector. Indices 0-78 retain the complete version 1
+contract; four nearest-dash values are appended at 79-82. It is privileged game state, not pixels.
+All values must be finite and normally lie in `[-1, 1]`; positive-only ratios lie in `[0, 1]`.
+
+### Ego state: 31 values
+
+| Indices | Encoding |
+| --- | --- |
+| 0-2 | Velocity projected onto track forward, left, and up, divided by 138.9 |
+| 3 | Racer speed divided by 138.9 |
+| 4 | Signed lateral track displacement divided by the applicable side radius, clipped to +/-1.25 and divided by 1.25 |
+| 5 | Track-normal displacement divided by the larger current radius |
+| 6-7 | Current left/right width encoded as `r / (r + 250)` |
+| 8-10 | Vehicle forward dotted with track forward, left, and up |
+| 11-12 | Vehicle up dotted with track up and left |
+| 13 | `energy / maxEnergy` |
+| 14 | Boost timer divided by 100 and clipped to `[0, 1]` |
+| 15 | `lapDistance / courseLength` |
+| 16 | Unwrapped race distance divided by total target race length, clipped to `[0, 1]` |
+| 17-21 | Can boost, airborne, any boost active, collision recoil, received damage |
+| 22-25 | One-hot current pit, dirt, dash, and ice contact; none is all zero |
+| 26-30 | Previous semantic action: normalized steer/pitch analog values and binary throttle/brake/boost |
+
+`Racer`, `SegmentPositionInfo`, and `CourseSegment` already contain these signals in
+`decomp/include/unk_structs.h`; racer flags are in `decomp/include/fzx_racer.h`. The track basis is
+x=forward, y=up, z=positive-left.
+
+### Course lookahead: 48 values
+
+Sample the course centerline at forward arc-length offsets:
+
+```text
+[250, 500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 8000]
+```
+
+Each of six samples contributes eight values:
+
+1. Future center position relative to the racer, projected into current track forward/left/up and
+   divided by the lookahead distance.
+2. Future track forward dotted with current track forward/left/up.
+3. Future left and right widths encoded as `r / (r + 250)`.
+
+The six lookahead samples remain geometry-only. Predictive dash information is supplied by the
+append-only descriptor below, without changing any version 1 index.
+
+### Nearest upcoming dash pad: 4 values
+
+| Index | Encoding |
+| --- | --- |
+| 79 | Valid bit. One when the course has at least one dash pad; otherwise all four values are zero. |
+| 80 | Wrapped forward arc distance to the next pad entry, divided by 8,000 and clipped to `[0, 1]` |
+| 81 | Pad target in `[-1, 1]`. Flat tracks use signed lateral center divided by the applicable side radius. Half-pipe, pipe, and cylinder tracks use signed wrapped surface angle relative to their neutral/middle pad angle, divided by 180 degrees. |
+| 82 | Pad half-width divided by the local half-track width (or tube radius), clipped to `[0, 1]` |
+
+Pad entries are sorted by absolute course distance at reset. The nearest query wraps around the
+closed course, so immediately after passing an entry it selects the next pad, including a pad on the
+next lap. Flat target centers undo the game's asymmetric-width collision-coordinate shift before
+normalization, keeping the value in the same positive-left convention as ego lateral displacement.
+Current contact remains the independent one-hot at index 24.
+
+Course export version 2 exposes the same sorted descriptors through
+`gdx_rl_export_dash_pads`. `GdxRlDashPad` is a 16-byte pointer-free record containing entry distance,
+normalized target, normalized width, and segment index. `GdxRlCourseMetadata.dash_pad_count` gives
+the required count, bounded by `GDX_RL_MAX_DASH_PADS=192`; a zero-capacity/null-storage export call
+queries that count without copying private course data to disk.
+
+The reference implementation performs a fixed-iteration wrapped arc-length inversion directly
+with the spline functions in `decomp/src/game/course.c` and does not allocate per step. Version 2
+caches the small dash descriptor table at reset and scans only those entries per observation. A
+fresh one-lap CPU-oracle run measured about 54,075 physics ticks/second versus the earlier rounded
+55,000-tick/s version 1 reference. A reset-time course lookup table remains an optional measured
+optimization; it must preserve observation parity within a documented tolerance.
+
+Other future surface effects, hazard distances, and nearby racers are later observation versions.
+Do not add opponents until solo completion works.
+
+The input-width change makes raw version 1 policy checkpoints structurally incompatible. A
+warm-start converter may expand the first encoder matrix from 79 to 83 columns, initialize the four
+new columns to zero, and copy every later tensor unchanged; this preserves the old policy exactly at
+the first version 2 rollout.
+
+## Reward and episode boundaries
+
+Accumulate reward on every 60 Hz physics tick and sum it across the action repeat:
+
+```text
+progress = (raceDistance_after - raceDistance_before) / 1000
+lap      = +2 when a new lap completes
+finish   = +25 once
+failure  = -25 once
+```
+
+Those remain raw core rewards and are accumulated in `episode_return` for diagnostics. On
+nonterminal transitions, the adapter adds any dash-contact shaping and applies the default
+`reward_scale=1/32` before writing to Puffer's rollout buffer. A scaled nonterminal reward outside
+`[-1, 1]` is a fatal contract violation rather than a silently clipped transition.
+
+For the one-lap pace task, the boundary transition instead uses one normalized outcome as its
+entire learner reward:
+
+```text
+successful score = clamp(finish_time_target_frames / episode_frame, 0, 1)
+fatal score      = -1  (spin, fall, crash, or retire)
+truncated score  =  0  (time limit or stall)
+```
+
+The default target is 2,000 physics frames. A zero target is the compatibility setting in which
+every successful finish scores 1. `score` and `perf` log this same terminal outcome, so lower
+successful lap times rank higher until the intentional ceiling at 1. `episode_return` remains the
+unscaled raw core-plus-shaping diagnostic, and `learner_return` records the actual mixture of
+scaled dense transitions and the exact terminal outcome. Terminal progress, lap, and dash shaping
+cannot perturb the `[-1, 1]` outcome contract.
+
+ABI v2 additionally reports `dash_pad_hits` in each transition. ABI v3 appends the four predictive
+dash observations and adds course-export version 2. The core increments `dash_pad_hits` once on an
+off-dash to on-dash contact edge inside the 60 Hz repeat loop, so a short pad cannot be missed by
+the final observation and a long contact cannot be paid every tick. The Puffer adapter's optional
+`dash_hit_reward` is zero by default and may add at most `+0.1` raw per hit. This is shaping only;
+the stock pad acceleration already contributes through distance progress. Episode logs report
+mean `dash_hits` independently of whether the shaping reward is enabled.
+
+`raceDistance = lapsCompletedDistance + lapDistance` is continuous over a forward lap boundary,
+so progress telescopes over an episode and reversing naturally produces negative reward. Do not
+clip normal progress, calculate a transition across reset, or add a speed/living reward. Log and
+fail tests on implausible teleports rather than hiding them with reward clipping.
+
+Do not penalize total energy loss: boost legitimately spends energy. If wall-banging becomes a
+learned exploit, add `-2 * damageTaken / maxEnergy` from the actual damage subtraction in
+`Racer_ReceiveDamage`.
+
+Set `terminated` on:
+
+- success: `RACER_STATE_FINISHED`;
+- failure: spinning out, falling off track, crashed, or retired.
+
+Set `truncated` on either:
+
+- the core default of 18,000 physics ticks (five simulated minutes); the one-lap Puffer curriculum
+  overrides this to 9,000;
+- no increase of the forward-progress high-water mark by at least 100 world units for 600 ticks,
+  measured only after controls are enabled.
+
+PufferLib 5.0 exposes one terminal float. The adapter tracks terminated and truncated separately
+for logs, then supplies terminal=1 for either so recurrent state resets. On an episode-ending
+`puf_step`, preserve the ending reward/terminal, reset immediately, and publish the new episode's
+initial observation, following the native Puffer auto-reset convention.
+
+## PufferLib 5.0 adapter
+
+PufferLib 5.0 is a native CUDA trainer, not the old Python extension. Registration is the presence
+of `ocean/fzero/fzero.h` and `config/fzero.ini`; build with `./build.sh fzero` and run
+`./puffer train ...`.
+
+The adapter defines:
+
+```c
+typedef float obs_t;
+#define OBS_SIZE 83
+#define ACT_SIZES {11, 5, 2, 2, 2}
+#define NUM_ATNS 5
+```
+
+Its `Env` contains one `Agent`, an opaque runtime handle, episode bookkeeping, and an all-float
+`Log`. `puf_init` sets `num_agents=1`, policy, and null action-mask pointer but does not touch IO
+buffers; Puffer wires those after initialization. `puf_reset` and `puf_step` write the buffers.
+
+The implemented `Log` contains normalized terminal `score`/`perf`, finish rate, raw episode return,
+actual learner return, decision and physics lengths, terminal race distance/speed/energy/lap, per-reason
+termination/truncation rates, mean-per-episode category fractions for steer/pitch/buttons, and `n`
+as the last float. Puffer treats it as a flat float struct and only aggregates records with nonzero
+`n`. Maximum-progress telemetry and race/lap times are planned additions for the next
+three-lap/full-head runs; invalid or nonfinite adapter inputs are fatal contract violations rather than episode
+statistics.
+
+Every env configuration key must exist in `config/fzero.ini` because Puffer 5.0 treats missing
+keys and unknown CLI overrides as errors. A workspace-relative path to the ignored private ROM may
+be the local default; `FZEROX_ROM` or an explicit override should take precedence for portability.
+Never commit the ROM itself. Smoke training sets `base.eval_episodes=0` so even the F-Zero
+overlay's configured eight-episode evaluation does not dominate a very short run.
+
+Puffer's training path supports a weights-only warm start through `base.load_model_path`. It loads
+policy 0 before the first rollout (and before any self-play pool snapshot); optimizer state and
+global step deliberately restart at zero. This is how a steering-curriculum checkpoint advances to
+the full five-head phase without discarding the learned observation encoder and steering policy.
+
+## Determinism and validation
+
+The semantic state hash contains values, never host pointers:
+
+- course and segment index/t;
+- position, displacement, velocity, true/track bases, speed, and height;
+- energy, boost and damage timers, racer flags, lap and race distances/times;
+- previous decoded action and raw controller current/pressed/released fields;
+- both game RNG states and gameplay frame count.
+
+When CMake is configured with `GDX_RL_TEST_ROM`, the private-ROM release suite is registered with
+CTest. The current suite has seven passing tests: smoke, contract, CPU oracle, public-observation
+one-lap and three-lap oracles, sequential worker-thread handoff, and dual-namespace isolation.
+The numbered audit also records separately run course-sweep and reset/thread stress evidence; those
+larger manual checks are not all CTest cases. Items marked **pending** below are additional
+hardening gates, not claims made by the seven-test suite:
+
+1. **Passed:** decode every endpoint/action ID and reject out-of-range or non-integral values.
+2. **Pending:** directly verify boost current/pressed/released fields on every repeated substep.
+3. **Passed:** verify finite bounded observations on all 24 courses, including course wrap.
+4. **Passed:** the same seed/action trace in two independently loaded instances gives equal state
+   and state hashes for at least 10,000 physics ticks.
+5. **Passed:** a cwd `gdx-autoinput.txt` and inherited `GDX_INPUT_SCRIPT` cannot alter an RL trace;
+   both legacy override paths are compiled out of the headless RL target.
+6. **Partly passed:** three-lap CPU return matches progress plus lap/finish bonuses. Directly test
+   that progress rewards telescope with no lap/reset spike and turn
+   negative while reversing.
+7. **Passed:** no-op truncates, held throttle gains progress/fails coherently, and the
+   built-in CPU controller finishes a lap.
+8. **Partly passed:** 1,000 equal-seed resets, 51 sequential thread migrations, adapter autoreset,
+   and nonfinite rejection pass. Longer leak stress and natural-intro parity remain pending.
+9. **Passed for namespaces:** two independent `dlmopen` modules show no cross-talk. A module rejects
+   second initialization; a larger process-worker backend remains pending.
+
+Pin the current aliasing/wrapping compiler flags and add `-ffp-contract=off` to the headless
+reference build. Same-build/same-machine traces are expected to be bit-identical. Cross-compiler
+and cross-architecture parity may use explicit tolerances later, but must never weaken the local
+determinism gate.
+
+## Current evidence
+
+The 2026-08-23 release-candidate checks establish both correct plumbing and a genuine one-lap
+learning baseline:
+
+- Equal-seed traces across two `dlmopen` namespaces stayed bit-exact for 10,001 physics ticks and
+  four completed CPU-oracle episodes. Repeated sequential worker-thread migration also retained
+  exact state.
+- The Mute City one-lap CPU oracle finished in 2,279 physics ticks. Single-instance throughput was
+  about 55,000 physics ticks/s, or about 13,750 policy decisions/s at repeat four.
+- A controller restricted to the public observation and legal action bins also establishes that
+  the interface contains enough information to drive. It finished one lap in 585 decisions/2,339
+  ticks and three laps in 1,740 decisions/6,960 ticks using only two lookahead values, lateral
+  velocity, binned steering, and forced throttle. It does not call the built-in CPU controller.
+- One thousand same-seed resets reproduced the same semantic hash. All 24 cartridge courses reset
+  with finite bounded observations. The stock CPU finished 22 of them; Mute City 3 and Port Town 2
+  are known CPU-controller limitations, so the oracle is not universal ground truth. Tested solo
+  Mute City action traces were also identical across different reset seeds; those seeds should not
+  be mislabeled as task generalization.
+- The Puffer adapter passed its action, curriculum-mask, worker-handoff,
+  terminal-preserving-autoreset, real-ROM, sanitizer, sweep, and native build checks. A real GPU
+  smoke executed eight PPO updates over 1,024 decisions, wrote eight distinct 445,440-byte
+  checkpoints, and reloaded the final checkpoint. It reported roughly 3,000 decisions/s and 1.3
+  GiB VRAM for a 111,360-parameter policy, but that short policy did not learn to finish.
+- A 65,536-decision steering-only run with learning rate 0.003, replay ratio 4, and entropy
+  coefficient 0.0001 learned repeated finishes in about 24.5 seconds. A fresh-process 64-rollout
+  evaluation finished 52 races (81.25%) with mean raw score 95.39. A weights-only continuation
+  loaded that checkpoint before the first rollout, trained for another 65,536 decisions at
+  learning rate 0.0015 and zero entropy coefficient in about 23.6 seconds, then finished 231 of
+  256 fresh-process stochastic rollouts (90.23%) with mean raw score 100.56.
+- That 256-rollout result clears the 90% gate by one finish and comes from one training seed and
+  one fixed sampler stream. It is a valid narrow pass, not yet a robust multi-seed estimate.
+- The exact no-op and untrained full-action exploratory baselines finished 0% of races; no-op
+  stayed near zero raw return and the untrained policy was around -20. The public-observation
+  oracle finished its tested one- and three-lap episodes; its one-lap raw score was 106.21.
+  Episode logs confirmed that the curriculum forced pitch neutral, throttle on, brake/boost off,
+  while all 11 steering-bin fractions were populated.
+- Evaluation samples the categorical policy with fixed RNG; it is reproducible but not greedy.
+  A zero-learning-rate warm-start check produced a checkpoint byte-identical to its source, proving
+  that the policy weights—not merely the path option—were loaded. Optimizer state, schedules, and
+  global step intentionally restart.
+- The fresh ABI-v3, 83-input run trained 819,200 decisions in 293.200 seconds. Its selected
+  step-786,432 checkpoint finished 64/64 exact stochastic evaluations in a mean 2,113.594 physics
+  frames, with normalized pace score 0.946328 and 2.625 dash contacts per lap. This beats the
+  fastest historical 79-input checkpoint by 15.312 frames on the identical evaluation stream.
+- The reduced CUDA simulator imports the ABI-v3 dash descriptors and matches observation indices
+  79-82 exactly in the current host full-lap parity test. It does not yet implement pad-contact
+  boost physics, contact counts, or `dash_hit_reward`; the ABI-v3 training result above uses exact
+  G-Diffuser CPU simulation with GPU PPO inference and optimization.
+
+## Milestones
+
+### M0: workspace and baselines
+
+- Keep all writes under the F-Zero workspace.
+- Keep the ROM private and untracked.
+- Pin isolated G-Diffuser and PufferLib 5.0 branches.
+- Record toolchain and dependency availability.
+
+### M1: buildable headless logic oracle
+
+- Add the cartridge-only headless target and minimal host shims.
+- Load the raw ROM with no first-boot UI, window, GL context, audio device/thread, save writes, or pacing.
+- Factor and test the renderer-independent host-address/asset resolver.
+- Generate the game's display lists but discard renderer execution while acknowledging tasks.
+- Reach one-car Time Attack through normal game flow.
+- Run the built-in CPU controller through the headless tick loop and finish one Mute City lap.
+- Report boot time, reset time, and simulator-only ticks/s.
+
+### M2: stable RL runtime
+
+- Add the versioned C ABI, action decoder, fast deterministic reset, 83-value observation packer,
+  reward components, terminal/truncation logic, state hash, and benchmark CLI.
+- Pass input-edge, repeat, reward, observation, replay determinism, and reset-stress tests.
+- Compare neutral intro fast-forward with the optimized intro skip.
+
+### M3: real PufferLib 5.0 smoke and one-lap learning baseline
+
+- Add `ocean/fzero/fzero.h`, `config/fzero.ini`, and a narrow build link to the already-built
+  runtime library.
+- Train one environment for at least 65,536 decisions with a small policy.
+- Save and evaluate a checkpoint against no-op and random baselines.
+- Confirm returns/progress change in the expected direction and inspect actual action histograms.
+
+### M4: scaling backend and three-lap/full-action baseline
+
+- Benchmark the one-runtime trainer, then select process servers, loader namespaces, or another
+  proven isolation backend. If process servers win, add a preallocated shared-memory protocol.
+- Benchmark 1, 2, 4, and 8 environments/threads; validate every reset and start, not only SPS.
+- Train the full five-head action space on three-lap Mute City.
+- Reach the 90%-over-256 reproducible-rollouts finish-rate gate, then define a separate task-variant
+  generalization gate.
+
+### M5: generalization and profiling
+
+- Add a small course curriculum, then all stock courses.
+- Sweep steering bins 7/11/21 and repeats 2/4/8.
+- Add Z/R controls only after solo reliability.
+- Profile reset, physics, observation packing, IPC, policy inference, copies, and learner time
+  separately.
+
+### M6: conditional acceleration
+
+Only if profiles show simulation is the limiting cost, optimize the proven CPU path. Candidate
+work proceeds from low risk to high risk: remove parity-proven update/draw/audio work, batch course
+lookahead/observation transforms, reduce IPC, then consider a purpose-built batched simulator.
+PufferLib 5.0 already trains the policy on CUDA; changing the simulator to CUDA is a separate
+project and is not justified by low Puffer SPS alone.
+
+## Immediate next steps
+
+1. Re-evaluate the one-lap checkpoint on several `base.seed` sampler streams and reproduce training
+   with three independent model seeds. Add greedy/argmax comparison when the evaluator supports it;
+   changing only `env.seed` does not create a tested task variant.
+2. Add maximum-progress and race/lap-time telemetry, direct boost-edge and reward
+   telescoping/reversal tests, and a longer reset/leak soak without changing the observation or
+   reward contract.
+3. Warm-start the proven steering policy on three-lap Mute City with `env.laps=3` and an explicit
+   `env.max_episode_frames=18000`, then apply the 90%-over-256-reproducible-rollouts gate. Keep the
+   one-lap checkpoint as a regression baseline.
+4. Benchmark 1, 2, 4, and 8 namespace-backed environments. Keep the existing one-environment path
+   as the reference and add process workers only when the namespace ceiling matters.
+5. After three-lap steering is reliable, warm-start with all five action heads exposed. Compare
+   against throttle-only and public-observation-oracle baselines and watch per-head action
+   histograms for collapse.
+6. Profile rollout, lookahead packing, inference, copies, and PPO separately. Optimize the CPU path
+   only from measured bottlenecks; do not begin a CUDA simulator rewrite based on aggregate trainer
+   SPS.
